<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lombards</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Langobards, Longobards</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> were a Germanic people who migrated from the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> by the early 6th century. In 568, under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alboin">Alboin</a>, they invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and founded a kingdom centred on Pavia, with powerful duchies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spoleto">Spoleto</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a>, which lasted until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s conquest in 774. The region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> keeps their name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lombards.jpg" alt="Lombards" width="320" height="406" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Gustav Droysen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Italien_zur_Langobardenzeit.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Roman historians of the 1st and 2nd centuries mention the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, or Langobardi, as one of the Suebian peoples and report them on the Lower Elbe in the early 1st century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>, writing about 100 CE, calls them a small people surrounded by the most powerful, who kept safe by daring the perils of war. A single mention in the Cosmographia of Julius Honorius is the only source for the 3rd and 4th centuries.</p><p>The standard explanation derives the name from Proto-Germanic words for long and beard. Three early medieval texts give mythical accounts of the migration, the oldest being the Origo Gentis Langobardorum. They say the people were first called Winnili and were ruled by the brothers Ibor and Aio, whose mother and adviser was Gambara.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> reappear in the late 5th century near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odoacer">Odoacer</a> defeated the Rugii in 487 or 488 they occupied Rugiland. King Audoin settled them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> and defeated the Gepid leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thurisind">Thurisind</a> in 551 or 552, and his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alboin">Alboin</a> destroyed the Gepid kingdom in 567. Near Szólád, archaeologists have found burials of Lombard men and women together as families, which was unusual among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a>.</p><p>In the spring of 568 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alboin">Alboin</a>, fearing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a>, led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, which the Gothic War had left depopulated and devastated. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suebi">Suebi</a>, Sarmatians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gepids">Gepids</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> and others joined them, and the invasion was almost unopposed. By late 569 they held all of northern Italy north of the Po except Pavia, which fell in 572. The kingdom reached its height under Liutprand in the 8th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> conquered it in 774, but Lombard nobles ruled the south into the 11th century, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> conquered them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> kings can be traced to about 380, and government is thought to have begun as an assembly that chose chiefs and war leaders from a nobility. The Lombard language is extinct, unless Cimbrian and Mòcheno are surviving dialects. Only fragments survive in runic inscriptions and in words quoted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> texts. St Barbatus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a> recorded that some Lombards venerated a golden viper and a tree. In their nomadic phase they made portable arms and jewellery, and settlement and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> brought churches and frescoes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lombards” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The catepanate governed a mixed population of Latin-speaking <b>Lombards</b> and Greeks, with Greek monasteries flourishing in Calabria.</blockquote><a href="/state/catepanate-of-italy">Catepanate of Italy</a></li><li><blockquote>When the <b>Lombards</b> entered Italy in 568, King Alboin left his nephew Gisulf at Forum Iulii (Cividale del Friuli) with chosen warrior families to hold the passes from the Julian Alps.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-friuli">Duchy of Friuli</a></li><li><blockquote>The duke led the local militia while the pope administered the vast Church estates, fed the city and negotiated with the <b>Lombards</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-rome">Duchy of Rome</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Lombards</b> occupied most of Tuscany in the 570s, the region was organised under dukes, with Lucca as the chief seat.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-tuscia">Duchy of Tuscia</a></li><li><blockquote>They seized Sirmium (Sremska Mitrovica) on the Sava, a key city, and competed with the Ostrogoths, Byzantium and the <b>Lombards</b> for the middle Danube.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-gepids">Kingdom of the Gepids</a></li><li><blockquote>By about 490 they had a strong kingdom that took tribute from the <b>Lombards</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-heruli">Kingdom of the Heruli</a></li></ul></section>
